A cached icon does not outlive the image that drew it

0020 made the cache's version depend on the flags that decide how an icon is
drawn, so that changing a flag throws the rows away. The icons themselves are
not a flag. They are files in the system image, and V80 changes all five of
them without changing anything 0020 counts.

What the reader saw: "进入首页首先还是上一轮的几个 ICON 一闪而过，然后变成了现在的
ICON" (2026-10-23). The old icons are not a leftover drawing; they are the
cached rows, and they are on the screen because of the order LoaderTask works
in -- loadWorkspace(), then bindWorkspace(), which is the first thing painted,
and only afterwards loadAllApps() and updateDbIcons(), which is where a row
whose package has changed is read again. So the first frame is always the
cache, and the correction always arrives after it.

IconCache cannot see that the icon files changed. updateDBIcons() asks the
package manager for each application's versionCode and lastUpdateTime, and the
system's own applications keep both across an update that replaces the image:
nothing in a row says which image drew it. The thing that does know is the
image's own identity, and the moment to act on it is before the first read
rather than after the first paint -- which is the IconDB constructor, since
IconCache is built before LoaderTask runs.

The clear is caught. clear() is the one method of SQLiteCacheHelper that does
not catch for itself, getWritableDatabase() throws on a full disk or a
database that did not survive the last shutdown, and leaving Android on this
device is a twenty-second hold of the power button -- so an uncontrolled
shutdown is the usual way out and a damaged cache is not a remote possibility.
An exception on this path reaches LauncherAppState and takes the home screen
down at every start, which is the failure this port has already had once
(0018, 0019). What catching it costs is one stale icon until the loader
corrects it, which is the behaviour before this patch.

The cost of the clear itself is one re-read of every icon and label on the
first start after an update -- the same cost 0020 pays, for the same reason,
and the start after an update is already the slow one.

diff --git a/src/com/android/launcher3/IconCache.java b/src/com/android/launcher3/IconCache.java
--- a/src/com/android/launcher3/IconCache.java
+++ b/src/com/android/launcher3/IconCache.java
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 import android.content.ContentValues;
 import android.content.Context;
 import android.content.Intent;
+import android.content.SharedPreferences;
 import android.content.pm.ActivityInfo;
 import android.content.pm.ApplicationInfo;
 import android.content.pm.LauncherActivityInfo;
@@ -785,10 +786,56 @@
         private final static String COLUMN_LABEL = "label";
         private final static String COLUMN_SYSTEM_STATE = "system_state";
 
+        /* PW3: which system image drew the rows below. The icons of this
+         * device's own applications are files in that image; replacing it
+         * replaces them, and a row carries nothing that says so -- the package
+         * manager reports the same versionCode and lastUpdateTime for a system
+         * application across an update, which is what updateDBIcons() compares.
+         *
+         * Not folded into RELEASE_VERSION above, where the flags live, because
+         * there is no room: the database's version is
+         * (RELEASE_VERSION << 16) + iconPixelSize and SQLiteOpenHelper refuses
+         * a version below 1, so RELEASE_VERSION has fifteen bits and five are
+         * spoken for. A build id hashed into the rest would collide, and a
+         * collision here is this bug again. */
+        private final static String KEY_IMAGE = "pw3_icon_cache_image";
+
         public IconDB(Context context, int iconPixelSize) {
             super(context, LauncherFiles.APP_ICONS_DB,
                     (RELEASE_VERSION << 16) + iconPixelSize,
                     TABLE_NAME);
+            /* PW3: here and not in updateDBIcons(), because of the order
+             * LoaderTask works in -- loadWorkspace(), then bindWorkspace(),
+             * which is the first thing painted, and only then loadAllApps()
+             * and updateDbIcons(). A row corrected there is corrected after
+             * the reader has seen the old icon. IconCache is built before any
+             * of that, so an empty table here is an empty table for the first
+             * paint.
+             *
+             * Caught, because this is a constructor on the path to the
+             * launcher's own: clear() is the one method of SQLiteCacheHelper
+             * that does not catch for itself (delete() and insertOrReplace()
+             * do), and getWritableDatabase() throws on a full disk or a
+             * database that did not survive the last shutdown. Leaving
+             * Android on this device is a twenty-second hold of the power
+             * button, so an uncontrolled shutdown is the usual way out and a
+             * damaged cache is not a remote possibility. An exception here
+             * would reach LauncherAppState and take the home screen down at
+             * every start, which is the failure this port has already had
+             * once (V78, Launcher3 0018/0019). What is lost by catching it is
+             * one stale icon until the loader corrects it: the behaviour
+             * before this patch. */
+            try {
+                SharedPreferences prefs = Utilities.getPrefs(context);
+                if (!Build.VERSION.INCREMENTAL.equals(prefs.getString(KEY_IMAGE, null))) {
+                    clear();
+                    /* Written only after the clear succeeded, so a failure is
+                     * retried at the next start rather than recorded as done. */
+                    prefs.edit().putString(KEY_IMAGE, Build.VERSION.INCREMENTAL).apply();
+                }
+            } catch (Exception e) {
+                Log.e(TAG, "PW3: could not clear the icon cache for a new system image", e);
+            }
         }
 
         @Override
